The normalised reveal time $r$ stays $O(1)$ while $\log_2 t$ grows linearly in the bit size: the
reveal time tracks $\sqrt p = N^{1/4}$, exponential in $\log N$. In every run the revealed
factor is exactly the prime whose reduced orbit closed first --- a direct confirmation of
Theorem~\ref{thm:revmin}.

\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{What is proved.} Within the $N$-explicit iteration model --- subsuming Pollard rho,
Pollard $p-1$, their polynomial variants, and every straight-line program over
$(+,-,\times,{}^{-1})$ with integer constants --- the factor-revealing event is uniquely an
exclusive one-sided CRT closure; the reveal time is exactly the smaller of the two closure
times; and each of the three exhaustive regimes costs $\Theta(N^{1/4})$ on average, or is
governed by an unknowable smoothness invariant, or is superpolynomial outright. Cycle detection
cannot beat the birthday exponent, and division cannot escape blindness without producing the
factorisation.

\paragraph{What is not proved.} This is not a lower bound for integer factorisation. The general
number field sieve is not an $N$-explicit iteration: it builds and sieves over algebraic number
fields and performs linear algebra over $\mathbb{F}_2$ on relations, with no counterpart in the
model. Nor does the argument touch quantum period-finding. Regime (b) shows the model itself
admits fast instances on special moduli, so no universal $\mathrm{poly}(\log N)$ lower bound
valid for all $N$-explicit maps and all $N$ is claimed --- such a statement would be equivalent
to the hardness of factoring.

\paragraph{Why the classification is exhaustive.} The reveal time is $\min(T_p,T_q)$, a closure
statistic of a deterministic map on a set of size $p$ or $q$. Any such map is either
statistically indistinguishable from a random map at the relevant scale (birthday regime),
endowed with group structure whose closure time is a multiplicative order (smoothness regime),
or so rigid that its orbit is a full cycle or a fixed point (simple regime).

\section{Future directions}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Beyond straight-line programs.} Branching programs --- computations that test a
value and take different paths --- are the natural next model. A conditional branch on ``is $v$
invertible?'' is exactly a factoring oracle call, so one expects the dichotomy to persist, but a
clean statement for multi-variable and randomised programs remains to be formulated.
\item \textbf{Distributional refinement of regime (a).} The average closure time is $\Theta(\sqrt
n)$ with explicit constants $1/2$ and $3$; matching the true constant $\sqrt{\pi/2}$ --- the expected rho
length of a random map --- from the exact birthday product is a concrete open problem.
\item \textbf{Non-uniform reduced dynamics.} Quadratic maps $x \mapsto x^2+c$ on $\Z/p$ are not
uniform; the extent to which their closure statistics match the uniform model is a longstanding
question in arithmetic dynamics, and any progress sharpens the regime-(a) barrier.
\item \textbf{The smoothness regime as a distributional statement.} Averaging the exact reveal
time over bases and over semiprimes with prescribed smoothness would turn regime (b) from a
pointwise identity into a distributional barrier.
\item \textbf{Second-order variants.} Iterations with memory, $x_{n+1} = f(x_n, x_{n-1})$, are
still CRT-blind by the same functoriality argument, but live on a larger state space; the
resulting change of birthday scale deserves quantification.
\end{itemize}

